{"id":"b1a25802-e54c-469d-a6de-5e05bacc94c9","object":"chat.completion","created":1784984157,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children_map = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        node_map[nid] = node\\n        children_map.setdefault(node['parent'], []).append(nid)\\n    root_id = None\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        if node['parent'] is None:\\n            root_id = node['id']\\n            break\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    retained = set(node['id'] for node in nodes)\\n    removed = []\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        if sym in required_symbols:\\n            other = False\\n            for other_id in retained:\\n                if other_id != nid and node_map[other_id]['symbol'] == sym:\\n                    other = True\\n                    break\\n            if not other:\\n                continue\\n        has_retained_children = any(child in retained for child in children_map.get(nid, []))\\n        if has_retained_children:\\n            continue\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed,\\n        'retained_node_ids': sorted(retained)\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a solve(case) function that takes a payload (the case object) and returns a JSON object with removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. The task is about grammar-guided node reduction on a parse tree. We have nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. The goal is to remove removable nodes while preserving required_symbols and ensuring grammar validity? The description mentions \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" and \"interestingness oracle\". But from the payload, we don't have a grammar or interestingness oracle explicitly. However, the instructions say to treat these as hard contracts: grammar_valid, interestingness_preserved. But we have no way to check them? Possibly the problem is a simplified version where the \"required_symbols\" defines the set of symbols that must remain present in the tree after removal. The \"interestingness\" might be simply that the required symbols are still present. Also \"grammar_valid\" might be that each node's symbol is consistent with parent? But we don't have grammar rules.\n\nGiven the fixture payload, the tree structure is given by parent references. The nodes have a \"removable\" boolean. Only nodes with removable=True can be removed. The root is not removable, and nodes with required symbols are likely not removable? In the fixtures, nodes with symbol \"root\" and \"s1\" are in required_symbols. Some nodes with \"s1\" are removable (n4 in first fixture). So perhaps the rule is: we can remove a node if it is removable, and after removal, all required symbols are still present somewhere in the tree. Also we need to consider deeper nodes first, and tie-break by node ID.\n\nBut also there is mention of \"replacement candidates\"? Possibly we can replace a node with its children? But the description says \"deletion and replacement candidates\". However, the output only requires removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. So maybe we just decide which nodes to remove.\n\nGiven that the objective is to reduce the tree as much as possible while preserving required symbols. The algorithm: iterate over nodes in order of decreasing depth (deeper first), for nodes at same depth sort by node ID (ascending? tie break). For each node that is removable, tentatively remove it, check if all required symbols still exist in the tree (i.e., at least one node with that symbol remains). If yes, mark it as removed; otherwise keep it. Then also need to ensure that we don't remove nodes that have retained children? The instruction says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" But that is contradictory to removal of internal nodes. Actually if we remove a node, we may need to remove its children as well? In tree reduction, removing a node might mean cutting the subtree. But the description: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" It doesn't say that children are removed. Possibly the removal is of the entire subtree? But the output has removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. So perhaps we are selecting a subset of nodes to keep, and the tree is defined by the set of retained nodes, but we need to ensure that the retained nodes form a valid tree? The description is ambiguous.\n\nLet's re-read: \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if we remove a node, we must also remove its children? Or we cannot remove a node if it has children that are retained? The phrase \"node with retained children\" suggests that we cannot remove a node if any of its children are retained; that implies that if we want to remove a node, we must first remove its children (deeper nodes first). So the algorithm is a bottom-up pruning: we can remove a node only if all its descendants have already been removed (or are being removed? Actually deeper nodes are considered first, so by the time we consider a node, its children will have been either removed or retained. If a child is retained, then we cannot remove the parent because it has a retained child. So the node can only be removed if all its children are removed. That makes sense: you prune subtrees starting from leaves.\n\nAdditionally, we cannot remove nodes with required symbols. So required_symbols indicate nodes that must remain. But it's a list of symbols, not node IDs. So we need to check that after removal, at least one node with each required symbol remains. That means we might not be able to remove all nodes of a required symbol if there is only one. In the fixtures, \"root\" and \"s1\" are required. root appears only once (root node), so root cannot be removed. \"s1\" appears multiple times: n1 and n4 in first fixture. n1 is not removable, n4 is removable. So we could remove n4 if after removal there is still a node with symbol \"s1\". That works because n1 remains.\n\nBut what about nodes that have both required symbol and removable? That's the case n4: removable=true, symbol=\"s1\". But n4 is a child of n2 (removable). If we remove n4, then n2 might later be considered. n2 is removable and its children? In first fixture, n2 has child n4 (removable) and maybe others? Actually n2 only has child n4? In the list, n2's parent is root, and n4's parent is n2. So n2 has one child n4. If we remove n4, then n2 has no retained children, so it could be removed later if allowed. But the algorithm considers deeper nodes first, so we process n4 before n2.\n\nAlso note the instruction: \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So sort by depth descending, then by node ID (ascending likely) to order processing.\n\nNow, after processing all nodes, we have a set of removed nodes (those we decided to remove) and retained nodes (the rest). But we also need to ensure that the tree is still connected? The root is always retained. The retained nodes should form a valid tree (each retained node's parent must also be retained? Not necessarily: if we remove a parent, we remove its entire subtree? But if we remove a parent, we must have removed its children first. So if we remove a node, we remove all its descendants as well. So the retained nodes will be a connected subtree containing the root. That is consistent.\n\nThus the algorithm:\n\n- Build a dict of nodes keyed by id.\n- Build a list of node ids sorted by depth descending, then by id ascending.\n- For each node in that order:\n    - if node is not removable: skip (cannot remove).\n    - if node.id == root: skip (root never removable anyway).\n    - Check if any required symbol would become absent if this node is removed. That is: for each required symbol, count occurrences in the retained set (initially all nodes). If the node's symbol is a required symbol, and it is the only node with that symbol among the current retained set, then removal would make that symbol missing -> cannot remove.\n    - Also check if any of its children are still retained? Actually children are deeper, so they have already been processed. If any child is still retained, then this node has retained children -> cannot remove (because \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\"). So we need to know which children are retained. We can maintain a set of retained node ids initially all nodes. When we decide to remove a node, we remove it from the retained set. So before deciding, we check if any child (direct children) is in retained set. If yes, cannot remove.\n    - If both conditions satisfied, we remove it: add to removed list, remove from retained set.\n- After processing all nodes, the retained set is what remains.\n- Output removed_node_ids: list of ids in the order they were removed (the order we processed them, but we only add when removed). The order should be the removal order, which is the processing order (depth descending, then id ascending) but only for those removed. That matches \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So removed_node_ids should be the list of node IDs in the order they were removed (i.e., as we remove them). retained_node_ids should be sorted (maybe alphabetically or by ID? The instruction says \"sorted retained IDs\". Likely sorted alphabetically or by ID. Since IDs are strings like \"n1\", \"n2\", etc., we can sort them lexicographically. In the output schema, items are strings, and \"sorted\" suggests we return them in sorted order. The requirement from the interface: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted, and removed_node_ids should be in the order of removal (not necessarily sorted). We'll implement that.\n\nEdge cases: root is not removable, required_symbols ensure at least one node with that symbol remains. For nodes with multiple children, if we remove a node, all its descendants are removed (since we already processed deeper nodes and if we removed them, they are gone; or if we didn't remove them, then those children are retained and parent cannot be removed). So the algorithm naturally prunes subtrees.\n\nNow, we need to write the solve(case) function. The case is the payload object containing \"nodes\" (list of dicts) and \"required_symbols\" (list of strings). It also has \"_case_nonce\" but we ignore that.\n\nWe'll implement as described.\n\nTest with first fixture:\n\nNodes:\n- root: depth0, removable false, symbol root\n- n1: depth1, parent root, removable false, symbol s1\n- n2: depth1, parent root, removable true, symbol s2\n- n3: depth1, parent root, removable false, symbol s0\n- n4: depth2, parent n2, removable true, symbol s1\n- n5: depth2, parent n3, removable false, symbol s2\n- n6: depth2, parent n1, removable true, symbol s0\n\nSorted by depth desc: depth2 nodes: n4, n5, n6 (order by id: n4, n5, n6)\ndepth1: n1, n2, n3\ndepth0: root.\n\nProcessing:\n- n4: removable true, symbol s1 (required). There are other s1: n1 (remains). So symbol ok. Children? n4 has no children (no node lists it as parent). So no retained children. So remove n4. retained removes n4.\n- n5: removable false -> skip.\n- n6: removable true, symbol s0 (not required). Check required symbols: root and s1 are still present. s0 not required, so fine. Children? n6 has no children. So remove n6.\n- n2: now depth1, removable true, symbol s2 (not required). Check children: n4 was removed, so no retained children? Actually n2's children list: only n4, and n4 is removed, so no retained children. So remove n2? But required symbols: need s1 and root. s1 present (n1). root present. So remove n2.\n- n1: removable false -> skip.\n- n3: removable false -> skip.\n- root: not removable.\n\nResult removed: [n4, n6, n2] (in order of removal). retained: root, n1, n3, n5? Also n? Wait, we removed n2, but n2's child n4 already removed. n5 is retained (it is child of n3). n3 is retained. So retained nodes: root, n1, n3, n5. Sorted IDs: n1, n3, n5, root? But root is id \"root\". Lex order: \"n1\", \"n3\", \"n5\", \"root\". That seems plausible.\n\nNow compare with expected? There is no expected output given, but this algorithm seems consistent with the description.\n\nOne nuance: The instruction says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" We correctly check that. Also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" We check that.\n\nThe algorithm also uses depth order and tie-break by node ID. We used ascending ID order; that matches typical interpretation.\n\nNow we need to write Python code for solve(case). Ensure we don't mutate input. Return dict with keys \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\". Use only standard library.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Extract nodes list and required_symbols set.\n2. Build dict nodes_by_id.\n3. Identify root node: node with parent null.\n4. Create list of all node ids sorted by (depth descending, id ascending). Use list comprehension.\n5. Initialize retained_set = set(all ids).\n6. removed_order = [].\n7. For each node_id in sorted_ids:\n    node = nodes_by_id[node_id]\n    if not node[\"removable\"]:\n        continue\n    if node_id == root[\"id\"]: # root never removable, but we check anyway\n        continue\n    # Check if removal would cause any required symbol to be missing\n    # Count occurrence of each required symbol in retained_set\n    # We can precompute symbol counts or just check per node.\n    # But we need to check if this node's symbol is the last one of that symbol.\n    # We can compute counts dynamically: for each required symbol, check if any node with that symbol remains other than this node. But careful: if node's symbol is required and it's the only node with that symbol in retained_set, then removing it would cause loss.\n    # We can compute the set of symbols from retained_set nodes, but need counts.\n    # Simpler: for each required symbol, we can check if there exists at least one node in retained_set (not this node) with that symbol. But if the node's symbol is not required, then it doesn't affect required symbols.\n    # So:\n    if node[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols:\n        # check if there is another node with same symbol in retained_set other than this node\n        other_found = False\n        for other_id in retained_set:\n            if other_id != node_id and nodes_by_id[other_id][\"symbol\"] == node[\"symbol\"]:\n                other_found = True\n                break\n        if not other_found:\n            # cannot remove\n            continue\n    # Check retained children: any child node (direct parent=this node) that is still in retained_set.\n    children_retained = any(child_id in retained_set for child_id in node_children.get(node_id, []))\n    if children_retained:\n        continue\n    # Remove\n    removed_order.append(node_id)\n    retained_set.remove(node_id)\n8. After loop, retained_node_ids = sorted(retained_set).\n9. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}.\n\nWe need to precompute children mapping: for each node, list children ids.\n\nEdge: root might not be in sorted_ids if we skip? We include all ids.\n\nAlso note: required_symbols list may contain symbols that are not present? But assume they are present.\n\nWe also need to ensure that we never remove root, but root is not removable anyway.\n\nNow produce the implementation as a JSON string with proper escaping.\n\nThe output should be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". The value is the Python source.\n\nWe'll write the code as a string.\n\nImplementation:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    # Build dict and children\n    node_map = {}\n    children_map = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        node_map[nid] = node\n        children_map.setdefault(node['parent'], []).append(nid)\n    # Identify root (parent None)\n    root_id = None\n    for node in nodes:\n        if node['parent'] is None:\n            root_id = node['id']\n            break\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    retained = set(node['id'] for node in nodes)\n    removed = []\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        if nid == root_id:\n            continue\n        # Check required symbols\n        sym = node['symbol']\n        if sym in required_symbols:\n            # Check if there is another node with same symbol in retained\n            other = False\n            for other_id in retained:\n                if other_id != nid and node_map[other_id]['symbol'] == sym:\n                    other = True\n                    break\n            if not other:\n                continue\n        # Check retained children\n        has_retained_children = any(child in retained for child in children_map.get(nid, []))\n        if has_retained_children:\n            continue\n        # Remove\n        removed.append(nid)\n        retained.remove(nid)\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": sorted(retained)\n    }\n\nWe need to ensure that the function returns a JSON-serializable object. This dict is fine.\n\nNow we need to encode it as JSON string. Use proper escaping.\n\nWe'll produce the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2139,"completion_tokens":4083,"total_tokens":6222,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1792},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3718},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1792,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":347},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}